    {
      "id": "workspace-bridge-apertures-landing-surface",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Shared Boundary Projection Experiment",
      "position": {
        "x": 120,
        "y": -1250
      },
      "width": 820,
      "height": 760,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        }
      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        }
      ],
      "inputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any"
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Shared Boundary Projection Experiment\n\n**Status: hypothesis under visual evaluation.**\n\nWhen a user activates an internal graph portal, Twilite adds or reuses the target graph beside the source graph. Workspace state binds the source portal to the target graph's resolved landing or delegated surface. If a compatible bridge supplies aperture geometry, the renderer places one shared projection in the gap between the frames.\n\nThe portal and target keep different graph-local IDs. The experiment treats them as two declarations participating in one addressed workspace presentation. The authored edge remains local and still names the portal. No graph document receives a cross-graph edge or workspace binding.\n\n## Trial behavior\n\n1. Click an internal graph portal.\n2. Open or reuse its target graph alongside.\n3. Resolve root, named-port, or node-specific exposure.\n4. Record a reversible workspace binding.\n5. Place an experimental shared surface at the matching bridge gap.\n6. Remove the binding when either graph closes.\n\n## Evaluation\n\nAccept the hypothesis only if the shared surface clearly communicates one relationship endpoint without hiding graph ownership. Rework or discard it if users cannot tell which graph owns the edge, which surface was selected, or how to return to the local portal.",
          "experimental": true
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "workspace-bridge-apertures-declaration"
        }
      }
    },
